You should be **alert about Gyan Bharatam, but not afraid of it**. In fact, after checking where it is in August 2026, I think it clarifies Pāṭala’s correct position.

The dangerous mistake would be trying to become “the biggest Sanskrit manuscript database with AI transcription/translation.” **India is now spending government-scale money on exactly that.** The opportunity is to become the **identity, provenance, scholarly-state and agent interoperability layer across all of those databases**.

As of July 27, 2026, Gyan Bharatam reported more than **1.19 crore manuscripts** identified through its national survey. Its portal already describes OCR/HTR, script recognition, semantic search, machine-assisted translation, Gyan Mitram, BharatiyaGPT/"Talk to Manuscript", and Pandulipi Darpan for manuscript-image recognition/transliteration/translation. The overall mission has an approved **₹482.85 crore 2024–31 budget** and explicitly plans APIs connecting national and international archives. ([Press Information Bureau][1])

That means **do not fight them for Layer 0. Make their success increase Pāṭala's value.**

## Why OpenPāṭala doesn't already exist

The interesting answer is that **most of it does exist—just as disconnected fragments**.

PANDiT is remarkably close to one portion of your Atlas vision: works, people, places, institutions, manuscripts, secondary literature and scholar-contributed curation across South Asian intellectual history. ([Digital Humanities Network][2])

SAMHiTA is assembling manuscripts of South Asian provenance held outside India into a relational database/archive, and explicitly says its information will be shared into PANDiT. ([SAMHiTA][3])

Muktabodha has more than 3,000 preserved Sanskrit texts, 570+ searchable e-texts, more than 2,000 IFP paper transcripts and enormous specialist Śaiva/Tantric holdings. ([Muktabodha][4])

SARIT already solved substantial TEI/text-encoding questions and publishes its corpus and encoding guidelines openly. ([CLARIAH-AT][5])

DCS provides a morphologically and lexically analyzed Sanskrit corpus; Sanskrit Heritage supplies segmentation/morphology/parser machinery; Vidyut provides modern Rust infrastructure for Sanskrit morphology and sandhi. ([Sanskrit Linguistics][6])

Ambuda is building the polished reading/library layer. ([Ambuda][7])

BDRC demonstrates what a properly institutionalized textual archive can eventually become: millions of digitized pages, cataloguing, research interfaces and IIIF-oriented infrastructure, now also moving toward AI datasets. ([BDRC][8])

And generic digital-humanities systems such as ResearchSpace already tackle heterogeneous cultural-heritage integration and perspective-preserving knowledge representation with CIDOC CRM. ([CIDOC CRM][9])

So the mystery is actually:

> Why has nobody connected **all of those layers into one continuously revisable scholarly memory**?

I think there are five reasons.

First, until very recently it was absurdly expensive. Extracting propositions, matching identities, tracking derivations, comparing translations, identifying conflicts and continually re-evaluating millions of objects implied enormous amounts of specialist human labour. Agents radically change those economics.

Second, institutional incentives are fragmented. A manuscript library's job is preservation. PANDiT's job is scholarly cataloguing. SARIT's job is electronic text. DCS's job is linguistic analysis. A publisher publishes editions. A scholar writes papers. OpenAlex indexes papers. Nobody is naturally rewarded for constructing the **epistemic connective tissue between everything**.

Third, ownership is fragmented. Gyan Bharatam itself explicitly notes that access depends on custodians, rights holders and applicable law. ([pib.gov.in][1]) That makes “copy everything into one universal repository” organizationally difficult.

Fourth, digital-humanities projects traditionally freeze an ontology around the grant/project they were created for. Your hardening document attacks precisely that failure mode by making observations and semantic history durable while treating ontology as a projection.

And fifth, the killer application wasn't obvious before AI. A painstaking provenance graph is academically nice. A provenance graph queried by **millions of autonomous research agents deciding which source to trust, what changed, what needs review and what evidence supports an answer** is infrastructure.

That last change is why this can make more sense now than it did ten years ago.

