## So should you be afraid?

**Of building the wrong product, yes.**

A generic Sanskrit repository, manuscript OCR platform, digital library, searchable text site or ChatGPT-for-manuscripts is rapidly being commoditized. Gyan Bharatam alone is enough evidence of that. ([pib.gov.in][1])

**Of Gyan Bharatam destroying the stronger Pāṭala vision, no.**

It may be one of the best things that could happen to it.

A government project can generate enormous quantities of scans, metadata, OCR and translations. It is much less obvious that a national digitization programme will become the neutral, cross-institutional, cross-country, cross-schema, scholar-adjudicated, append-only **epistemic memory layer** connecting Gyan Bharatam to PANDiT, German e-texts, French manuscript collections, Oxford scholarship, modern publications and eventually Greek corpora.

That is where I would plant the flag:

> **OpenPāṭala is not the archive. It is the memory and research protocol over the archives.**

And your hardening proposal finally gives that claim a credible technical meaning rather than making it branding.

The strategically valuable thing to lock down now is therefore not more Sanskrit ontology. It is the **provider/observation/assertion/identity/adjudication protocol plus adapters**. Once that works, Gyan Bharatam, SAMHiTA, PANDiT, Muktabodha, BDRC, SARIT, OpenAlex and whatever appears in 2030 become plugins feeding the same machine rather than competitors you need to outrun. ([Ministry of Culture][18])

